Nexus-BS: Interrupt on Async Advance handled as Linux ehci-q.c / ehci-timer.c.

- IAA cycles (start_iaa_cycle/end_iaa_cycle): an unlinked asynchronous QH waits for the next
  doorbell; only the end of a cycle started after its unlink hands it back. The interrupt
  handler takes IAA before USBINT/USBERR, so a QH unlinked in the same pass is never completed
  by an IAA that answered an earlier doorbell (stock: every unlinked QH completed on any IAA,
  then freed and reused while the controller could still fetch it).
- One doorbell per handler pass for all QHs it unlinked (batched unlinks).
- IAA watchdog (ehci_iaa_watchdog): a cycle older than CONFIG_USB_EHCI_IAA_WATCHDOG_US ends
  anyway, counted as lost (IAA without interrupt, IAAD dropped) or late; never a transfer error.
- usbh_kill_urb(): unlink and join the cycles, wait (polling, then 1 ms sleeps) for the
  hand-back; no PSE/ASE toggling, no private handshake that cleared USBSTS.IAA under other
  QHs, no QH freed before its IAA, never "iaad timeout"/-USB_ERR_TIMEOUT. Periodic QHs wait
  two microframes. A QH not on its list is not "unlinked" (stock walk relinked the periodic
  tail or looped forever on the ring).
- Bulk toggle after a kill or transaction error comes from the (stable) QH overlay.
- CONFIG_USB_EHCI_DMA_WMB(): descriptor stores drained (DSB + outer cache sync on the
  Zynq) before linking a QH, before ASE/PSE writes and before the doorbell, as Linux wmb() /
  writel(). USBCMD set without writing IAAD back (Linux writes its shadow ehci->command).
- The QH semaphore is reset on allocation; a synchronous URB that finished between its
  timeout and the kill reports its own result.

